<section>
  <grid spacing="none">
    <grid-item colspan="38" vertical-align="center">
      <h2>One recovery story, two different refrigerant issues</h2>
      <p>Stratospheric ozone absorbs harmful ultraviolet radiation. The Montreal Protocol phases out ozone-depleting substances.</p>
      <grid divider="sm neutral-border" vertical-sizing="hug">
        <grid-item colspan="20" vertical-align="center">
          <display size="md">2040</display>
          <h4>Global</h4>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item colspan="20" vertical-align="center">
          <display size="md">2045</display>
          <h4>Arctic</h4>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item colspan="20" vertical-align="center">
          <display size="md">2066</display>
          <h4>Antarctica</h4>
        </grid-item>
      </grid>
      <p font-size="sm"><b>Projected return to 1980 ozone levels—if current policies remain.</b></p>
      <aside variant="info">
        <p><b>Keep separate:</b> HFCs warm the climate but do not deplete ozone. The Kigali Amendment is related, but scientifically and policy-distinct.</p>
      </aside>
      <p role="caption">Sources: <a href="https://www.unep.org/news-and-stories/press-release/ozone-layer-recovery-track-helping-avoid-global-warming-05degc">UNEP 2023 assessment summary</a>; <a href="https://ozone.unep.org/treaties/montreal-protocol">Montreal Protocol</a>.</p>
    </grid-item>
    <grid-item colspan="22" padding="0">
      <ai-image background="true" aspect-ratio="portrait" image-style="industrial-atmospheric-relief" prompt="Precision editorial relief cross-section of layered stratosphere above an abstract refrigeration system, a compressor and coiled tubing connected beneath translucent mineral-paper atmospheric bands, oxidized metal details, cool raking light, exposure blue and transition teal with one restrained rust signal, no text, no lettering, no logos"/>
    </grid-item>
  </grid>
</section>